+/* Ceyx project patch 09: block-parallel Fuji strip decode.
+
+   Upstream parallelises fuji_decode_loop() only under OpenMP. This project
+   builds OpenMP ON for desktop and OFF for mobile/iOS/Android
+   (CEYX_ENABLE_DESKTOP_OPENMP, native/cmake/tests.cmake), so BOTH paths must
+   work: the OpenMP branch is upstream's, and the std::thread pool in the
+   #else branch keeps the 11-way strip parallelism of an X-T5 lossy RAF on any
+   toolchain without OpenMP. The pool is confined to a single unpack() call
+   and always joins before returning. */
+#ifndef LIBRAW_USE_OPENMP
+#include <atomic>
+#include <exception>
+#include <thread>
+#include <vector>
+#endif
+
+/* Deliberately NOT inside the #ifndef above: the datastream sites this guards
+   are unsynchronised in upstream's OpenMP build too, and this project now
+   ships that build.
+
+   Upstream wraps only the seek+read pair in fuji_fill_buffer() in an
+   `omp critical`. It leaves init_fuji_block()'s input->size() and both
+   derror() calls unguarded, even though derror() probes the stream (eof/tell)
+   and increments unpacker_data.data_error, all shared across strip workers.
+
+   A std::mutex rather than another `omp critical` on purpose: derror() can
+   throw LIBRAW_EXCEPTION_IO_EOF, and an exception must not escape an OpenMP
+   structured block, so `omp critical` is not a legal way to guard it. A
+   lock_guard unwinds correctly. No deadlock against the omp critical region:
+   the two never nest (init_fuji_block releases this mutex before calling
+   fuji_fill_buffer), and they are only ever taken one at a time.
+
+   Cost is negligible: a 64 KiB refill roughly once per XTRANS_BUF_SIZE bytes
+   of input, against the entropy decoding it serialises with. */
+#include <mutex>
+static std::mutex fuji_decode_stream_mutex;
+
